# Contributing to OpenDCKit

Thanks for your interest in OpenDCKit. Everything the project does happens in the open and
anyone is welcome to take part.

This file is the CONTRIBUTING file referred to in the
[OpenDCKit Technical Charter](https://github.com/opendckit/governance/tree/main/charter). The charter gives it a specific job: it documents the
project's roles, and it sets out who the Maintainers are, which in turn determines the
voting membership of the Technical Steering Committee. The TSC may amend this file to
implement its own policies, within the bounds the charter sets.

> **Draft.** Links and contacts marked TBD are set up during onboarding.

## Code of conduct

The [LF Projects Code of Conduct](https://lfprojects.org/policies/code-of-conduct/) applies
to everyone participating in OpenDCKit, unless and until the TSC adopts a project-specific
code of conduct with the approval of the Series Manager. Report concerns to the TSC chair or
to conduct@lfprojects.org.

## Roles

The charter defines Contributors and Maintainers, and allows the TSC to document additional
roles here. OpenDCKit uses three.

**Contributors** are anyone in the technical community who contributes code, documentation,
or other technical artifacts to the project. No approval or paperwork is needed to start.
Participation is open to anyone who abides by the charter.

**Maintainers** are Contributors who have earned the ability to commit to a project
repository. They review and merge pull requests, triage issues, and look after the health of
the areas they cover. Maintainer status is granted per repository, and each repository lists
its maintainers in its `MAINTAINERS.md` file.

**TSC voting members** sit on the Technical Steering Committee, the technical governing body
of the project, responsible for all technical oversight. The TSC is separate from the LF
Networking Governing Board, which handles budget, marketing, and other non-technical
matters. TSC voting members are drawn from the Maintainers. The roster, chair, and term
dates are kept in [`TSC.md`](https://github.com/opendckit/governance/blob/main/TSC.md) in
the governance repository.

Being a Maintainer does not make someone a TSC voting member. Maintainer status is a
prerequisite for a TSC seat, not the seat itself, and most Maintainers will not hold one.

The charter's default is that every Maintainer is a TSC voting member, and it allows the
project to use a different approach so long as that approach is documented in this file.
The separation described here is that documented approach.

**At inception.** The initial TSC voting members are the people listed in
[`TSC.md`](https://github.com/opendckit/governance/blob/main/TSC.md) when the charter is
adopted. Under Section 2.b of the charter they are seated as the
project's initial Maintainers for TSC purposes, whether or not they hold commit rights in a
repository yet. They are appointed because the project does not yet have a Maintainer
community to elect a TSC, and they serve until the first TSC election. The requirement that
a TSC voting member be a repository Maintainer applies to seats filled after inception.

## TSC voting members

The current roster and chair are listed in
[`TSC.md`](https://github.com/opendckit/governance/blob/main/TSC.md).

Seats are held by individuals, not by companies. A member who changes employer keeps their
seat.

### Joining the TSC

After inception, TSC seats are filled by election. The Maintainers are the electorate: at
each TSC election, Maintainers nominate candidates from among the Maintainers, including
themselves, and then vote. The sitting TSC does not appoint its own members.

The Linux Foundation administers each election as a neutral party. LF staff open the
nomination period, run the vote, and record the result. The Maintainers nominate and vote.
Once an election closes, the result is recorded by pull request in the
[governance repository](https://github.com/opendckit/governance), updating `TSC.md` and
adding the election record under `elections/`.

*Number of seats, term length, election cadence and the date of the first election, how the
electorate is counted (for example one vote per person however many repositories they
maintain), the voting method, and how a mid-term vacancy is filled: to be defined by the TSC
before the first election. See "Policies for the TSC to define" below.*

### Leaving the TSC

A member may step down at any time by pull request against `TSC.md`. A member may be
removed by a vote of the TSC under the decision-making rules below. After inception,
losing Maintainer status in every repository ends eligibility for the seat.

*Grounds for removal, inactivity thresholds, and emeritus status: to be defined by the TSC.*

## Becoming a Maintainer

Maintainers are added per repository, and the decision starts with the people who maintain
that repository.

1. **Nomination.** An existing Maintainer of the repository opens a pull request adding the
   Contributor to that repository's `MAINTAINERS.md`, with a short case for the nomination.
2. **Repository vote.** The existing Maintainers of that repository vote. A majority of them
   carries the nomination. This is where the substantive decision is made; they are the
   people who know the candidate's work.
3. **TSC approval.** The carried nomination goes to the TSC, which approves it by majority
   vote as the charter requires. This is a sign-off rather than a re-run of the decision.
   The TSC is expected to approve a nomination the repository has carried, and would
   normally only decline for a project-level reason such as a code of conduct matter or a
   conflict with an org-wide policy. A declined nomination comes back to the repository with
   the reason.
4. **Merge.** Once the TSC has approved, the pull request merges and commit rights are
   granted.

*How the repository vote is run and recorded, any quorum for it, and whether the TSC may
delegate its approval: to be defined by the TSC.*

### New repositories

When the TSC creates a repository, the proposal may include an initial list of Maintainers.
Approving the repository approves that list, and no separate nomination round is needed. The
list lands in the new repository's `MAINTAINERS.md` when it is created. If a repository is
created without one, the TSC appoints the initial Maintainers.

### Leaving

A Maintainer may step down at any time by pull request against that repository's
`MAINTAINERS.md`. Removal follows the same path as a nomination: a majority of the
repository's Maintainers, then approval by the TSC.

*Promotion requirements, inactivity thresholds, and emeritus status: to be defined by the
TSC.*

## How to contribute

1. Open an issue first for anything larger than a bug fix, so the design is discussed before
   the code is written.
2. Fork the repository and work on a branch.
3. Keep pull requests focused. One logical change per pull request reviews faster than a
   large mixed one.
4. Include tests for behavior changes, and update documentation in the same pull request.
5. Sign off every commit (see below).
6. Open the pull request and respond to review comments.

### Sign-off (DCO)

Every commit must carry a Developer Certificate of Origin sign-off. Use `git commit -s`,
which appends a line like:

```
Signed-off-by: Jane Doe <jane@example.com>
```

By signing off you certify the statement at <https://developercertificate.org>. The name and
email must match the commit author.

<!-- TODO (LF staff): confirm whether a contributor license agreement will be required in
addition to the DCO sign-off. The draft charter (Section 7) specifies DCO only, so a CLA
needs confirming with LF before it is documented here. -->

If you forget, `git commit --amend -s` fixes the last commit and `git rebase --signoff`
fixes a branch.

### Review and merge

Pull requests need approval from at least one repository maintainer other than the author.
Repository maintainers do not merge their own pull requests without another maintainer's
approval. If a reviewer requests changes, that objection is resolved before merge rather
than outvoted.

Disagreements the repository maintainers cannot resolve among themselves go to the TSC.

## TSC decision making

The project aims to operate as a consensus-based community. Where a decision requires a
vote, it is one vote per voting member. Quorum is at least half of all voting members. At a
meeting with quorum, decisions carry on a majority of those in attendance. Electronic votes
without a meeting require a majority of all voting members. License exceptions and charter
amendments require a two-thirds vote of the entire TSC. The charter governs if this summary
and the charter ever disagree.

TSC meetings are open to the public and may be held electronically, by teleconference, or in
person.

These are the charter's baseline mechanics. Eligibility to vote, limits on how many seats
one company may hold, term lengths, and similar matters are not addressed by the charter and
are for the TSC to define here.

## Licensing

Code contributions are made under the [Apache License 2.0](LICENSE), the Project License.
Documentation is made available under
[CC BY 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). Contributors retain copyright in
their contributions; nothing is assigned to the project.

Add an SPDX identifier to new source files:

```
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
```

Any dependency added must be compatible with the Project License. Use of an alternative
license for an inbound or outbound contribution requires an exception approved by a
two-thirds vote of the entire TSC.

Code brought into the project, including existing code imported into a new repository, goes
through the Linux Foundation license and security scan first. Any dependency that downstream
users need for the software to work must pass the same scan. Dependencies used only for
building or testing are reviewed but held to a lighter standard. Do not commit prebuilt
third-party binaries; consume them as declared dependencies instead.

Original contributors are credited in the commit history and, where appropriate, in a
NOTICE file. Project documentation describes OpenDCKit as a community project and does not
describe it as owned by any one company.

## Reporting a security issue

Do not open a public issue for a suspected vulnerability. Report it to TBD. The community
will acknowledge the report and coordinate disclosure with the reporter.

## Where the community talks

- Chat: <https://linuxfoundation.zulipchat.com>, in `#lfn-opendckit-discussion`,
  `#lfn-opendckit-support` and `#lfn-opendckit-tsc`. Sign in with your LF ID.
- TSC meeting: weekly on Mondays at 07:00 America/Los_Angeles. Agendas and minutes are on
  the [OpenDCKit wiki](https://lf-networking.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/OpenDCKit/overview).
- Meetings: all public meetings are on the
  [Community Meeting Calendar](https://zoom-lfx.platform.linuxfoundation.org/meetings/opendckit?view=week).
  To request a new meeting, email <support@lfnetworking.org> with the meeting name, its
  committee, working group, or SIG (if any), the cadence, and the time, including the time
  zone (UTC, PT, etc.)
- Mailing list: <https://groups.linuxfoundation.org/g/opendckit-formation> until the project
  lists are created
- LF Networking: OpenDCKit coordinates with other LF Networking projects through the
  [LFN Technical Advisory Council](https://lf-networking.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/LN/pages/15633036/TAC+Technical+Advisory+Council).
- Issues and pull requests: <https://github.com/opendckit>
- Infrastructure and tooling requests: <support@lfnetworking.org>

Technical discussion happens in the open. Decisions made in a meeting or a chat are
summarized back into the relevant issue or pull request so that people who were not present
can follow along.

## Policies for the TSC to define

The charter sets baseline voting mechanics and leaves the rest to the TSC. Until the TSC
adopts a policy, none of these constraints apply beyond what the charter itself requires.

These belong in this file, because they govern contributing and how Maintainers and TSC
voting members are determined:

**Maintainer eligibility.** What qualifies a Contributor for nomination. Whether there are
minimum contribution, tenure, or review requirements, and whether they are objective
thresholds or TSC judgment.

**Delegation of maintainer approval.** Whether the TSC keeps approval of new Maintainers or
delegates it to the existing Maintainers of each repository.

**Maintainer terms.** Whether repository Maintainers serve until resignation or removal, or
for fixed terms with renewal.

**TSC elections.** How many voting seats the TSC has, the length of a term and whether terms
are staggered, how often elections are held and when the first one takes place, how the
Maintainer electorate is counted, the voting method, and whether a mid-term vacancy is
filled by special election or left open until the next election. These need to be settled
before the first election, which ends the terms of the members appointed at inception.

**Company and affiliate caps.** Whether a limit applies to how many TSC voting seats a
single company and its affiliates may hold, what the limit is, and how a breach is resolved
when it arises through an affiliation change rather than a new seating. The founding group
has proposed a cap of two voting seats per company, moved here from an earlier charter
draft, for the TSC to confirm and word.

Policies on how the TSC itself operates, such as chair election, voting practice, attendance,
conflicts of interest, working groups, and releases, are kept in the
[governance repository](https://github.com/opendckit/governance/tree/main/policies).

## Changing this file

The TSC may amend this file by the voting rules above, including adjusting roles, setting
requirements for promotion, and documenting an alternative approach for determining TSC
voting membership, as the charter permits. Amendments are made by pull request.
